-- Records the content gap the "America's War On Residential Solar" review surfaced
-- (Two Bit da Vinci, 12 Sep 2026): the site has twelve guides on electricity and
-- none on what any of it costs or what the law allows. Verified 2026-09-15 that
-- nothing under src/ mentions price per watt, tariffs, tax credits, net metering
-- or plug-in solar, and that no existing board row covers them.
--
-- Two new guide rows, and three existing rows amended with reasoning they were
-- carrying implicitly. No cost calculator: that was ruled a non-goal in CLAUDE.md
-- in this same change, which also retires half of the sizing chain's "NOT DONE".
--
-- Deliberately NOT deadline-shaped. Both topics arrived with dates attached --
-- SB 868's 30 September signature deadline and the 4 December tariff -- and both
-- were dropped, because publishing needs a public site and Phase 0 still has the
-- LLC, insurance and outside legal review open at 0%. What is left is the durable
-- half of each topic, which is the half that keeps earning after the date passes.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

insert into public.roadmap_items
  (phase, category, title, description, status, dev_percent_complete, is_public, display_order)
values
  (1, 'onboarding', 'Guide: plug-in solar — the 1,200 W on-ramp',
   'Differentiator #1 applied to the lowest barrier to entry in solar: a UL-certified system at or below 1,200 W that plugs into an ordinary outlet and is exempt from interconnection agreements, utility approval and fees in the states that have adopted the template. Utah HB 340 went first, signed March 2025, defining a category for portable solar generation devices up to 1,200 W. Eight more states have followed as of September 2026 -- Maine, Virginia, Colorado, Maryland, Connecticut, Vermont, New Hampshire and New Jersey -- and California''s SB 868, the Plug and Play Solar Act, cleared the Assembly 73-0 and was delivered to the governor on 31 August 2026. SCOPE EXCEPTION, RECORDED DELIBERATELY: plug-in solar is grid-parallel, neither off-grid nor hybrid, so it sits outside the audience line the Blue Ocean contract draws. It is admitted anyway as the beginner funnel -- the person who starts with one balcony panel is the person the sizing chain is built for two years later -- and admitted in writing, as an exception made on purpose rather than drift found afterwards. If it stops feeding the rest of the site, it goes. LEGAL SHAPE: this is a guide, never a widget. A control that takes a state and outputs "legal for you" is the Jeppesen chart shape the legal posture rules out, because it converts data into an output acted on directly in a regulated activity. Cite the statute, date the claim, and send the reader to their AHJ. DELIBERATELY NOT A DEADLINE ITEM: SB 868 carried a 30 September 2026 signature deadline, which was considered and dropped. Publishing needs a public site, and Phase 0 still has the LLC, liability insurance and outside legal review open at 0%. The durable half -- which states, what the rules are, what the UL anti-islanding requirement means -- outlives the news peg and grows as more states adopt.',
   'planned', 0, true, 103),
  (1, 'onboarding', 'Guide: what a solar system should cost',
   'Differentiator #1 applied to the question every beginner asks first and that the site cannot currently answer at all: as of 15 September 2026 nothing under src/ mentions price per watt, tariffs, tax credits or payback. Seeds differentiator #3 as well -- a $/W benchmark is the first aggregate the community layer can carry ("systems like yours came in at X/W"), which is why this is built as a reference page with a table rather than prose. WHAT IT CARRIES. (1) THE INSTALLED-PRICE GAP. The US pays roughly three times Australia''s installed price for identical hardware, and the cause is not the hardware. Customer acquisition alone ran 23% of a residential system''s price at $0.75/W in H1 2021, more than twice what the module cost that year; marketing, profit and labour together are around 43% of an American system, while modules are about 12% of what a homeowner pays. (2) A REAL BILL OF MATERIALS, so the number is not abstract: 20 x 450 W panels at $130, five 4-in-1 micro-inverters at $330, $2,000 of racking, wire and hardware, $500 of permits and $3,000 of labour -- a 9 kW build at roughly $1.05/W against about $3/W installed. (3) THE IMPORT PRICE FLOOR, and the finding that makes this page ours rather than a rewrite of everyone else''s tariff post. The Section 232 proclamation of 6 August 2026 sets a minimum import price of $0.38/W on modules and $0.22/W on cells, plus a 15% ad valorem duty, effective 12:01 a.m. ET on 4 December 2026. Against a median transacted price near $0.271/W that is about 40% on the module. On a full installed system it is roughly 2.7%, which is why the trade press treats it as a rounding error -- but this site''s reader buys bare pallets, where there is no $28,000 denominator to dilute it. The one audience for whom the floor is a real number is the one nobody is writing for. (4) THE 30% CREDIT IS GONE. P.L. 119-21 section 70506 terminated 26 U.S.C. 25D for expenditures made after 31 December 2025, and 25D already treats an expenditure as made when the original installation is completed -- so paying in 2025 for a 2026 install earns nothing. No cost figure anywhere on this site may assume 30% off. COST STAYS IN GUIDES: the non-goal added to CLAUDE.md alongside this item rules out a payback or $/W calculator, for the Winter-versus-Jeppesen reason the legal posture already sets out. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH: the Section 232 figures were confirmed 15 September 2026 against pv magazine USA and Anza. Trade remedies get amended -- re-check and re-date every number on this page at publish time.',
   'planned', 0, true, 104);

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'Initial version shipped 2026-09-01 as /calculators/system, step 7. The item asked for one flow with assumptions carried visibly, uncertainty shown where it enters, and a stated confidence band -- and named the failure it exists to prevent: "four tools that each look authoritative and quietly disagree is the worst configuration available." That is not hypothetical. Panel sizing rounds UP to a whole count from an energy target; array wiring must factor that count into whole strings inside a voltage window and a current limit. For the SG550WM against an SPH10048P at a -25.9 degC design low, EVERY ODD COUNT fails -- 9, 11, 13 and 15 have no safe arrangement while 10, 12, 14 and 16 do. The panel page says buy 11, the array page says nothing fits, and neither is wrong on its own terms. src/lib/system-design.ts does three things no single step structurally can: DISAGREEMENTS (the panel-count mismatch, a battery and inverter at different voltages, an array past the PV input, string fusing needed or unknown, panels sized but never wired), each with what each side says, why neither is wrong alone, what to do, and links back to both steps; ASSUMPTIONS WITH PROVENANCE -- every figure that shapes the result, which step set it, whether it is still the shipped default, what it changes downstream and how much it moves the answer, ranked by leverage rather than by step; and a CONFIDENCE BAND that is deliberately a word rather than a percentage, because a numeric interval would need error distributions this app does not have. A value equal to the shipped default reads as "still at the default", never "you did not choose this". 2026-09-02, from direct feedback: THE CABLE STEP COULD NEVER BE COMPLETED. It was the only step that publishes nothing, because it is a tool run repeatedly -- once per cable run -- rather than a step with one answer, so every other step could be detected done and this one never could. The chain dead-ended there. It now publishes a ProtectionSummary keyed by run: the user picks a gauge from the set that passed (the conductor output still refuses to name one -- that is the protection register, and recording which one was CHOSEN is the honest way to remember a decision the calculator declined to make), saves it, and the page shows "N of M runs sized" with what remains. Completion means every applicable run has a gauge, which is why the combiner run is excluded when there is one string per tracker. The system page gained a cable schedule -- run, current, length, gauge, drop and the protecting device -- which is the first real step toward the bill of materials. A drift affordance was added at the same time: redoing an upstream step leaves the cable fields holding yesterday figures, so when they disagree with the chain the page offers the current ones rather than silently overwriting, matching the suggestedDuty pattern. Also fixed: confidence() no longer excludes protection as permanently undetectable. NOT DONE: printable bill of materials and cost estimate. WHY THE CHAIN IS BATTERY-FIRST, recorded 2026-09-15 because it was the chain''s unstated premise: exporting to the grid no longer pays for a system. NEM 3.0 cut California export credits by roughly a factor of seven, so about 7 kWh sent at noon buys back 1 kWh at 8 p.m., and SDG&E moved its super-off-peak window out to 2 p.m., putting the heart of solar production at the cheapest price of the day -- which penalises the households that have solar and rewards the ones that do not. Sizing an array to export is sizing against the tariff. Sizing it to charge a bank that carries the 4-to-9 p.m. peak is the case that still holds, and that is the order the chain already walks; this records why. SCOPE CORRECTION: the previous description closed with "NOT DONE: printable bill of materials and cost estimate", listing two things as remaining work. Only the bill of materials remains. The cost estimate was retired on 2026-09-15 as an explicit non-goal in CLAUDE.md -- a calculator that converts a user''s inputs into a payback or $/W figure is the Jeppesen chart shape, not the Winter shape, and cost belongs in the guides instead.'
  where title = 'System designer: one integrated sizing chain';

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'The user-facing live monitoring screen and the ingest endpoint the local agent posts to. Depends on the dashboard shell. Free feature — see "Local monitoring agent". Paid tiers are deferred until there is evidence users will pay, and are not assumed by the plan. THE CUSTOMER STORY, added 2026-09-15: orphaned systems. The industry''s own term for an install whose company has gone under -- no monitoring, no warranty, nobody to call -- with thousands of lawsuits pending against solar finance companies on exactly this. Panels last 25 years and micro-inverters around 20, so the hardware reliably outlives the business that sold it, and a 25-year workmanship warranty is worth only as much as the company that wrote it. That is the sharpest argument differentiator #2 has and it was written down nowhere: a brand-agnostic agent that speaks MODBUS to whatever is on the wall keeps working when the vendor''s cloud is switched off. Proprietary monitoring cannot make that promise -- it is the failure mode. Use this framing on the monitoring landing copy when it is written.'
  where title = 'Monitoring dashboard UI + /api/ingest';

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'Opt-in anonymized aggregate stats ("systems like yours averaged X peak sun hours"). Differentiator #3. FIRST AGGREGATE, decided 2026-09-15: installed cost in $/W, broken down by system size and region. It is the easiest number for a builder to report accurately -- they know what they paid -- it needs no telemetry and no agent install, so it can ship long before the monitoring phases, and it is the one figure the whole market is opaque about. The "what a solar system should cost" guide is written as its seed: a reference table that starts as one documented build and becomes a distribution once builders report into it.'
  where title = 'Community data aggregation';
